    "---\n",
    "## Part 2: Attractors and Basins of Attraction\n",
    "\n",
    "### Background: State Space and Attractors\n",
    "\n",
    "Because the state space is finite ($2^n$) and the update rule is deterministic, every trajectory must eventually enter an **attractor**.\n",
    "\n",
    "There are two fundamental types:\n",
    "1. **Fixed Point:** The network reaches a state and stays there ($x^*(t+1) = x^*(t)$). Biologically, this represents a stable cell state like G1 arrest or apoptosis.\n",
    "2. **Limit Cycle:** The network oscillates through a set of states. The period is the number of states in the cycle. Biologically, this corresponds to cell cycle progression or circadian rhythms.\n",
    "\n",
    "The **basin of attraction** of an attractor is the set of all initial states that eventually flow into it. A large basin implies a robust cell fate; a small basin implies a fragile state."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
