    "\n",
    "**Tasks:**\n",
    "1. Complete the `find_attractor(start, transitions)` function. It should follow the trajectory from `start` until it loops back to a previously visited state. Return the attractor (the set of states in the cycle).\n",
    "2. For each of the 8 initial states, find its attractor.\n",
    "3. Group the initial states by their attractor to compute the **basin of attraction** for each.\n",
    "4. Print a summary: for each attractor, how many states are in its basin?\n",
    "5. **Interpret:** Is the fixed point or the cycle more robust? (i.e., which one has the larger basin?)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Hint:** Use a dictionary to store the index of each visited state. When you revisit a state, the attractor starts at that index."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def find_attractor(start, transitions):\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    Follow the trajectory from `start` until it loops back, and return the attractor.\n",
    "    \n",
    "    Parameters:\n",
    "    start : tuple — initial state\n",
    "    transitions : dict — mapping from state to next state\n",
    "    \n",
    "    Returns:\n",
    "    tuple of states — the attractor (fixed point or cycle)\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    visited = {}\n",
    "    path = []\n",
    "    curr = start\n",
    "    while curr not in visited:\n",
    "        visited[curr] = len(path)\n",
    "        path.append(curr)\n",
    "        curr = transitions[curr]\n",
    "    # Your code here — return the attractor (the cycle portion of the path)\n",
    "    return None\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "# Your code here — compute attractors for all 8 states and group by basin\n",
    "\n",
    "# Your code here — print the basin sizes"
