    "---\n",
    "## Part 3: Asynchronous Updates\n",
    "\n",
    "### Background: Why Asynchronous?\n",
    "\n",
    "Synchronous updates assume every node updates at exactly the same time, which is rarely true in a real cell. **Asynchronous updates** are more realistic: at each time step, one node is chosen at random and updated based on the current state of its regulators.\n",
    "\n",
    "Asynchronous updates can reveal attractors that synchronous updates miss, and they often change the system's robustness. A state that is a stable fixed point under synchronous updates may no longer be stable under asynchronous updates (or vice versa).\n",
    "\n",
    "**The update rule:**\n",
    "- Pick a random index $i \\in \\{A, B, C\\}$.\n",
    "- Update only $x_i$ using its Boolean rule, leaving the other two unchanged."
   ]
  },
